    <statement><p>Calculate the integral approximations <m>T_{6}</m> , <m>M_{6}</m> and <m>S_{12}</m> for</p>
<p><m>\int^{4}_{0} x^{5} \, dx</m>.</p>
<p><m>T_{6}</m> = <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p><m>M_{6}</m> = <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p><m>S_{12}</m> = <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>729.811</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>659.193</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>682.732</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>f(x) = x^{5}</m>.  We divide [0, 4] into 6 subintervals of width



<m>\Delta x = \frac{4 - 0}{6} = 0.666667</m>



with endpoints <m>x_i</m> = 0, 0.666667, 1.33333, 2, 2.66667, 3.33333, 4, and midpoints <m>c_j</m> = 0.333333, 1, 1.66667, 2.33333, 3, 3.66667.  With this data, we get



<m>T_{6} = \frac{1}{2} \Delta x \left( f(x_0) + 2  f(x_1) + ... + 2  f(x_{N-1}) + f(x_N) \right) = \frac{1}{2} \cdotp 0.666667 \left( 0 + 0.263374 + 8.42798 + 64 + 269.695 + 823.045 + 1024 \right) = 729.811</m>



<m>M_{6} = \Delta x \left( f(c_1) + ... + f(c_N) \right) =  0.666667 \left( 0.00411523 + 1 + 12.8601 + 69.1646 + 243 + 662.761 \right) = 659.193</m>



<m>S_{12} = \frac{2 M_{6}+T_{6}}{3}  = 682.732</m>


</solution>
